      content:
          '- **Sickle Cell History**: Diagnosed at birth via newborn screening. Genotype HbSS. Two prior vaso-occlusive crises (ages 4 and 5), both managed with IV fluids and analgesics. No prior acute chest syndrome, splenic sequestration, or stroke. Baseline hemoglobin 7.5-8.0 g/dL.\n- **Stroke Prevention Screening**: TCD screening performed 3 months ago showed right MCA velocity 210 cm/s (abnormal >200 cm/s — indication for chronic transfusion therapy per STOP trial). Hematology scheduled chronic transfusion initiation, but the family missed the appointment.\n- **Current Stroke**: Acute onset left-sided weakness and slurred speech while at school. Teacher called 911. CT head negative for hemorrhage. MRI: acute right MCA territory infarction involving frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. MRA: moyamoya-pattern collaterals with severe right ICA stenosis.\n- **Hospital Course**: Exchange transfusion in PICU (target HbS <30%). Started on chronic simple transfusion protocol. Neurosurgery consulted for possible future revascularization (encephaloduroarteriosynangiosis — EDAS).\n- **Current Functional Status**: Left hemiparesis — arm 2/5, leg 3/5. Left hemispatial neglect present. Dysarthria — intelligible to familiar listeners only. Requires max assist for transfers and ADLs. Continent but needs help toileting. Emotional — cries frequently, asks when she can go home.\n- **Developmental History**: Previously meeting all milestones. Attended first grade. Right-hand dominant. Enjoyed dance class. Social and outgoing personality.\n- **Family**: Parents are from Guatemala. Father works in construction (day labor). Mother is primary caregiver. Two younger siblings (ages 3 and 1). Family lives in a one-bedroom apartment. No health insurance — care funded through emergency Medicaid. Parents are undocumented immigrants.\n- **Medications**: Hydroxyurea 500 mg daily (resumed), folic acid 1 mg daily, chronic transfusion protocol (target HbS <30%, Hgb 9-10 g/dL).',
