    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the pathophysiology of stroke in sickle cell disease, the significance of the missed TCD screening follow-up, and the secondary prevention strategy.',
      content:
          '**Stroke Pathophysiology in Sickle Cell Disease**:\n- Children with HbSS have an **11% risk of overt stroke** by age 20 (highest risk ages 2-9).\n- **Large-vessel vasculopathy**: Repeated sickling causes endothelial damage, intimal hyperplasia, and progressive stenosis of the distal ICA and proximal MCA/ACA. This creates a moyamoya-pattern collateral network.\n- **Mechanisms**: (1) Arterial stenosis leading to hypoperfusion and watershed infarction; (2) Thrombosis at stenotic segments; (3) Impaired vasodilatory reserve due to chronic anemia and endothelial dysfunction.\n- This patient\'s moyamoya-pattern vasculopathy with right ICA stenosis is classic.\n\n**Significance of Elevated TCD Velocities**:\n- The **STOP trial** (1998) established that TCD screening identifies children at high stroke risk.\n- **Normal**: <170 cm/s. **Conditional**: 170-199 cm/s. **Abnormal**: >=200 cm/s.\n- Children with abnormal TCD (>=200 cm/s) have a **40% stroke risk over 3 years** without intervention.\n- **Chronic transfusion therapy** reduces stroke risk by **>90%** (from 10%/year to <1%/year) — the STOP trial was halted early due to dramatic benefit.\n- This patient had a TCD of **210 cm/s** — meeting criteria for chronic transfusion. The missed appointment was a critical preventive care failure. **However**, blame must be contextualized: the family faces language barriers, lacks insurance, has transportation challenges, and lives in fear of immigration enforcement.\n\n**Secondary Prevention Strategy**:\n1. **Chronic transfusion therapy**: Simple or exchange transfusions every 3-4 weeks. Target: HbS <30% of total hemoglobin, maintain Hgb 9-10 g/dL. This is **lifelong** — the STOP II trial showed that discontinuing transfusions leads to reversion of TCD velocities and recurrent stroke risk.\n2. **Iron chelation**: Chronic transfusions cause iron overload. Begin chelation (deferasirox) when ferritin >1000 or after approximately 1 year of transfusions. Monitor ferritin and liver iron concentration.\n3. **Hydroxyurea**: Continue as adjunct. The TWiTCH trial showed hydroxyurea can substitute for transfusions in primary prevention if TCD normalizes — but this patient has had a stroke, so transfusions remain the standard of care for secondary prevention.\n4. **Revascularization surgery**: Neurosurgery consultation for EDAS (encephaloduroarteriosynangiosis) — indirect revascularization procedure that promotes collateral growth. Indicated for progressive moyamoya vasculopathy with recurrent ischemia despite transfusion.\n5. **Screening**: Repeat MRI/MRA in 3-6 months. TCD on contralateral (left) side. Screen for silent cerebral infarcts (present in 30% of SCD children, associated with cognitive decline).\n6. **Cognitive screening**: Neuropsychological testing when stabilized — SCD children have high rates of executive dysfunction, processing speed deficits, and academic difficulties even without overt stroke.',
