    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a pediatric rehabilitation plan that accounts for her developmental stage, sickle cell-specific needs, and family circumstances.',
      content:
          '**Pediatric Stroke Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Motor Recovery (PT/OT)**:\n- **Constraint-induced movement therapy (CIMT) — modified pediatric protocol**: Restraint of right (unaffected) hand with a mitt for structured play sessions (2-3 hours/day) to force use of left arm. Shaping tasks: reaching for toys, stacking blocks, grasping crayons. Evidence supports CIMT in pediatric stroke with significant UE improvement.\n- **Task-oriented training**: Functional activities meaningful to a 6-year-old — dressing a doll (bilateral hand use), coloring/drawing, feeding herself, putting on shoes.\n- **Left leg strengthening**: Progressive weight-bearing activities — standing at a play table, stepping over obstacles, kicking a ball. Ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) for left foot drop (ankle 2/5).\n- **Balance training**: Seated balance on therapy ball, progressing to supported standing, then dynamic standing activities.\n- **Subluxation management**: Arm trough when in wheelchair. Shoulder support during upright activities. No overhead pulleys or aggressive PROM.\n\n**2. Neglect Remediation**:\n- **Visual scanning training**: Games that require looking left — finding hidden objects on the left side of a picture, turning pages of a book (items placed on left).\n- **Prism adaptation therapy**: Emerging evidence in children — brief prism goggle sessions followed by pointing tasks to recalibrate spatial attention.\n- **Environmental cueing**: Place preferred toys, food, and visitors on the left side. Red tape line on left edge of tray as visual anchor.\n- **Left-sided sensory stimulation**: Vibration, textured toys, and tactile games targeting left arm and leg.\n\n**3. Speech-Language (SLP)**:\n- **Dysarthria treatment**: Oral motor exercises (lip/tongue strengthening). Rate control strategies. Over-articulation practice with age-appropriate games (animal sounds, singing).\n- **Language assessment**: Assess in Spanish (primary language). Ensure bilingual SLP or interpreter-mediated therapy. Evaluate for receptive and expressive language — right MCA stroke can affect pragmatic language and prosody.\n- **Swallowing**: Confirm safe oral intake. Modified diet if needed based on clinical swallow evaluation.\n\n**4. Sickle Cell-Specific Considerations**:\n- **Hydration**: Maintain excellent hydration (1.5-2x maintenance IV fluids or oral equivalent) to prevent sickling. Monitor I&O.\n- **Temperature regulation**: Avoid cold therapy. Keep room warm. Cold can trigger vaso-occlusive crises.\n- **Pain monitoring**: Use age-appropriate pain scale (Wong-Baker FACES). Daily assessment for vaso-occlusive pain.\n- **Infection prevention**: Ensure vaccination status is current (especially pneumococcal). Penicillin prophylaxis (standard for SCD children — check if she is on it).\n- **Transfusion schedule**: Coordinate rehabilitation around transfusion days (every 3-4 weeks). She may be fatigued on transfusion days.\n\n**5. Psychological/Developmental Support**:\n- **Child life specialist**: Daily visits. Therapeutic play to process the hospitalization experience. Normalize hospital environment.\n- **Psychology**: Assessment for adjustment disorder. Trauma-informed play therapy. Address separation anxiety (cries when mother leaves).\n- **Education**: Hospital-based teacher to maintain academic progress during rehabilitation stay. IEP (Individualized Education Program) planning for school reentry.\n- **Family-centered care**: Include parents in all therapy sessions. Teach them exercises to continue at home. Provide education materials in Spanish.\n\n**6. Discharge Planning**:\n- **Equipment**: Pediatric wheelchair (may be temporary), AFO, bath chair, adaptive utensils.\n- **Home therapy**: Outpatient PT, OT, SLP — ensure providers accept emergency Medicaid and are Spanish-speaking or have interpreter services.\n- **School reentry**: Occupational therapist to visit school, recommend modifications (left-sided desk setup, extra time, aide for mobility). 504 plan or IEP.\n- **Follow-up**: Hematology (transfusion clinic), PM&R, neurosurgery, neuropsychology.',
