    Section(
      title: '+ How do you ensure continuity of care for a child with sickle cell disease requiring lifelong transfusion therapy when the family is uninsured and fears immigration enforcement?',
      content:
          '**Addressing Healthcare Access Barriers**:\n\n**1. Insurance and Coverage**:\n- **Emergency Medicaid**: Currently covering the inpatient stay. This covers emergency services but NOT routine outpatient care, chronic transfusions, or rehabilitation in most states.\n- **CHIP (Children\'s Health Insurance Program)**: The child, if born in the US, is a **US citizen** regardless of parental immigration status and is eligible for full Medicaid or CHIP. If born abroad, CHIP eligibility varies by state. Verify her citizenship/birth status.\n- **State-specific programs**: Many states offer full Medicaid to children regardless of immigration status (e.g., California, New York, Illinois, Washington, Oregon). Social worker must determine state-specific eligibility.\n- **Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs)**: Required to serve all patients regardless of insurance or immigration status on a sliding-fee scale. Identify the nearest FQHC for primary care.\n\n**2. Immigration Concerns**:\n- **Hospitals are sensitive locations**: ICE (Immigration and Customs Enforcement) policy designates hospitals, schools, and churches as sensitive locations where enforcement actions are generally avoided.\n- **HIPAA protections**: Patient information cannot be shared with immigration authorities without a court order.\n- **Public charge rule**: As of current policy, Medicaid use by US citizen children does NOT count against parents in public charge determinations. The social worker should provide this information clearly.\n- **Connect with immigrant advocacy organization**: Legal aid organizations that specialize in immigrant healthcare access can provide the family with written assurance of their rights.\n\n**3. Transfusion Therapy Access**:\n- The child needs **lifelong monthly transfusions** — this is a life-or-death treatment. Missing transfusions means recurrent stroke risk.\n- **Sickle cell comprehensive centers**: Federally funded sickle cell treatment centers provide comprehensive care and often have social workers dedicated to navigating access barriers.\n- **Hospital financial assistance/charity care**: Most hospitals have charity care programs for uninsured patients. Apply proactively.\n- **Sickle Cell Disease Association of America (SCDAA)**: Provides resources, advocacy, and case management support.\n\n**4. Language Access**:\n- **Professional medical interpreters**: Required by law (Title VI of the Civil Rights Act) for all clinical encounters. Do not rely on the child or family members as interpreters.\n- **Written materials in Spanish**: Discharge instructions, medication guides, follow-up schedules, and home exercise programs.\n- **Spanish-speaking providers**: When possible, match the family with Spanish-speaking therapists and physicians for continuity.\n\n**5. Transportation**:\n- **Non-emergency medical transportation (NEMT)**: Covered by Medicaid in most states. Arrange for monthly transfusion appointments.\n- **Hospital-based van services or partnerships with rideshare programs**: Many children\'s hospitals offer transportation assistance.\n\n**6. Care Coordination**:\n- Assign a **bilingual social worker or patient navigator** as the family\'s single point of contact.\n- Create a **written care calendar** in Spanish with all appointment dates, locations, and contact numbers.\n- **Hematology-PM&R communication**: Ensure both teams are aligned on the schedule so rehabilitation appointments do not conflict with transfusion days.',
