    Section(
      title: '+ How do you address this family\'s fear of immigration enforcement while ensuring their child receives the lifelong medical care she needs?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge the fear directly and validate it.**\n[Through the interpreter, speaking slowly and making eye contact with both parents]\n"I hear you, and I want you to know that your fear is understandable. What happened to your neighbor is real, and I am not going to tell you it is nothing to worry about. You are trying to protect your family, and that is exactly what good parents do."\n\n**2. Provide concrete factual reassurance about hospital safety.**\n"I want to tell you something important about this hospital. This is a safe place. Immigration authorities have a policy of not conducting enforcement at hospitals, schools, or churches. We do not ask about immigration status. We do not share your information with immigration. Your medical records are protected by federal privacy law — no one outside this hospital can access them without a court order. I want you to hear that clearly: coming to this hospital for your daughter\'s care will not put you at risk."\n\n**3. Address the specific fear that caused the missed appointment.**\n"I understand that fear is what caused you to miss the blood transfusion appointment. I am not blaming you for that — I blame a system that made you feel unsafe seeking medical care for your child. But I need to be honest with you about what that missed appointment means. Your daughter\'s blood test three months ago showed that she was at very high risk for a stroke. The transfusions we wanted to start would have reduced that risk dramatically. She had her stroke because we did not get the chance to protect her. That is not your fault — it is a tragedy that should not have happened. But now, going forward, she needs these transfusions every month for the rest of her life to prevent another stroke. If she has another stroke, she could lose the ability to walk, to talk, or worse."\n\n**4. Address the child\'s distress.**\n[Look at the daughter and smile gently. Speak to her in simple terms through the interpreter]\n"Sweetie, your mommy and daddy are talking to the doctor because they want to make sure you get better. Everything is okay. Do you want to color while we finish talking? [Offer crayons and paper] Your mommy and daddy love you very much, and we are all going to take care of you."\n\n**5. Offer concrete protections and connect to resources.**\n[Back to parents] "Here is what I am going to do. I am going to connect you with a social worker who speaks Spanish and who helps families in your situation every day. She can connect you with a lawyer — free of charge — who specializes in immigrant rights and healthcare access. She can explain your legal protections in detail. We are also going to help you apply for insurance for your daughter — she may qualify for full coverage that pays for everything she needs. And if transportation is a barrier, we will arrange rides to every appointment."\n\n**6. Reframe: their presence is essential to their daughter\'s survival.**\n"You said that if you are deported, your daughter has no one. You are right. She needs you. And she needs you to be brave enough to bring her to her appointments, because those transfusions are what will keep her alive and healthy. I know that is a lot to ask when you are afraid. But I promise you that this medical team will do everything in our power to make sure seeking care for your daughter is safe for your family."\n\n**7. Establish a trusting relationship.**\n"I want to be your daughter\'s doctor for a long time. I want to watch her get stronger, go back to school, and go back to dance class. To do that, I need you to trust me enough to keep coming back. Can we make a plan together?"\n\n**Key Principles**: Validate fear without dismissing it. Provide factual reassurance about hospital safety policies. Be honest about the medical consequences of the missed appointment without blaming. Offer concrete resources (social worker, legal aid, insurance navigation, transportation). Address the child\'s emotional experience. Build a long-term therapeutic alliance grounded in trust.',
